# Installation instructions

How to install the gitea-to-local-git synchronizer on a new machine, step by step. The order matters: prerequisites, files in place, credentials, systemd service, cron, verification.

The commands below use placeholder values. Substitute the real ones for the target machine:

- `<user>` — the Linux username that owns the Git repositories to be synced (also the user the service will run as).
- `<user-home>` — that user's home directory (usually `/home/<user>`).
- `<install-dir>` — the absolute path where this `gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer/` directory lives on disk.
- `<gitea-host>` — the hostname or IP of the Gitea instance.
- `<gitea-port>` — the port Gitea listens on (default `3000`).
- `<gitea-username>` — the Gitea account whose repositories are kept in sync.
- `<gitea-api-token>` — an API token issued to that account.


## Prerequisites

### On the sync machine

1. Linux with systemd and cron. Any modern distribution works.
2. Command-line tools installed: `bash`, `inotify-tools` (provides `inotifywait`), `sqlite3`, `curl`, `python3`, `git`. On Debian/Ubuntu:
   ```
   sudo apt install bash inotify-tools sqlite3 curl python3 git
   ```
3. A Linux user account that owns the Git repositories to be synced (that's `<user>` below).
4. Avoid `<user-home>` paths containing spaces — `SCAN_ROOTS` is word-split on whitespace.

### On the Gitea side

1. A running Gitea instance reachable from the sync machine over HTTP or HTTPS.
2. An account with an API token that has `repo` write permission. Generate the token in Gitea's web UI: Settings → Applications → Generate New Token. **Record the token immediately — it is shown only once.**
3. If the sync machine will auto-import local repositories into Gitea (i.e. repositories that exist locally but not yet on Gitea), Gitea must allow migrations from local/private addresses. In Gitea's `app.ini`:
   ```
   [migrations]
   ALLOW_LOCALNETWORKS = true
   ```
   Restart Gitea after changing this. Skip only if you will create every repository manually in Gitea before adding it on disk.

### If Gitea runs on the same box as the synchronizer

The synchronizer reads bare repositories directly from `GITEA_REPOS_DIR` (default `/var/lib/gitea/data/gitea-repositories`) and writes to Gitea's SQLite database (`GITEA_DB`, default `/var/lib/gitea/data/gitea.db`). Both paths are owned by the `gitea` user by default. The service user `<user>` must be able to read the bare repos and read/write the database.

The simplest route:

```
sudo usermod -a -G gitea <user>
sudo chmod g+rwX /var/lib/gitea/data/gitea.db /var/lib/gitea/data/gitea-repositories
sudo find /var/lib/gitea/data/gitea-repositories -type d -exec chmod g+rwx {} \;
sudo find /var/lib/gitea/data/gitea-repositories -type f -exec chmod g+rw {} \;
```

Log out and back in (or restart the service after Step 4) so the new group takes effect.

### If Gitea runs on a different host

This synchronizer **requires filesystem access** to Gitea's bare repositories and SQLite database. The core sync fetches from `GITEA_REPOS_DIR/<user>/<repo>.git` directly on disk, not over the Gitea HTTP API, and the auxiliary features (DB reconcile, pull-request pruning) also read and write the Gitea data directory.

Options when Gitea runs on a different host:

- Mount Gitea's data directory on the sync machine (NFS, sshfs, etc.) and set `GITEA_REPOS_DIR` / `GITEA_DB` in `/etc/gitea-sync.env` to the mount paths. The mount must be read-write; the service user must have read access to the bare repos and read-write access to the database file.
- Or run the synchronizer on the Gitea host itself.

A pure-remote, API-only mode is not supported.


## Step 1: Place the files

Copy or clone the `gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer/` directory into a stable location on disk. That location is `<install-dir>` below.

Make the three scripts executable:

```
chmod +x <install-dir>/scripts/bidirectional-sync <install-dir>/scripts/ref-change-watcher <install-dir>/scripts/orphan-ref-pruner
```


## Step 2: Set up the credentials file

This file holds the Gitea URL, Gitea username, Gitea API token, and the folders to scan for Git repositories. It lives in `/etc/` because it holds a secret.

Create `/etc/gitea-sync.env` with content like this (all four variables are required):

```
GITEA_URL=http://<gitea-host>:<gitea-port>
GITEA_USER=<gitea-username>
GITEA_TOKEN=<gitea-api-token>
SCAN_ROOTS=<user-home>
```

`GITEA_URL` must include the scheme. Both `http://` and `https://` are supported — the scheme you supply is preserved in all push and clone URLs. `SCAN_ROOTS` is a space-separated list of root directories under which the synchronizer searches recursively for `.git` folders (excluding `node_modules`, `.cache`, virtualenvs, and similar — see `EXCLUDE_REGEX` below).

Optional overrides (defaults shown):

```
GITEA_REPOS_DIR=/var/lib/gitea/data/gitea-repositories
GITEA_DB=/var/lib/gitea/data/gitea.db
EXCLUDE_REGEX=/(node_modules|\.nvm|\.cache|\.local/share|vendor|\.venv|venv)/
```

`GITEA_REPOS_DIR` and `GITEA_DB` must resolve to the actual Gitea data paths reachable from this machine; if Gitea is on another host, these must point at the mount where its data directory is exposed (see "If Gitea runs on a different host" above).

Lock the file down so only root and `<user>` can read it:

```
sudo chown root:<user> /etc/gitea-sync.env
sudo chmod 640 /etc/gitea-sync.env
```


## Step 3: Review user-settings.conf (optional)

The file `<install-dir>/support-artifacts/user-settings.conf` holds the tunable knobs: debounce time, rescan interval, heartbeat interval, log cap, Gitea API page size, and the `AUTO_PRUNE` toggle. The defaults are sensible for most users. Each setting has a plain-language comment explaining what it does. Edit any values worth changing; skip this step to run on defaults.


## Step 4: Install the systemd service

This runs `ref-change-watcher` continuously in the background, surviving reboots and restarting on crash.

Create `/etc/systemd/system/gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service` with:

```
[Unit]
Description=Gitea-to-local-git synchronizer — event-driven two-way sync watcher
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=<user>
Group=<user>
EnvironmentFile=/etc/gitea-sync.env
ExecStart=<install-dir>/scripts/ref-change-watcher
Restart=always
RestartSec=5
# Script errors go to the activity log; keep stdout/stderr out of journald.
StandardOutput=null
StandardError=null

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

If Gitea runs on this same box under the stock `gitea.service` unit, append `gitea.service` to both `After=` and `Wants=` so the watcher starts after Gitea is up.

If `<user>` is not in a user-private group (i.e. a group with the same name as the user), substitute an appropriate group name for `Group=<user>`.

Load, enable, and start it:

```
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service
sudo systemctl start  gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service
```


## Step 5: Install the cron entry for the pruner (optional)

This runs `orphan-ref-pruner` every 5 minutes as a safety net. `bidirectional-sync` also triggers the pruner immediately whenever anything gets deleted, so this is mostly a background cleanup.

Skip this step if `AUTO_PRUNE=false` is set in `user-settings.conf` — the operator has opted to manage repository garbage collection themselves.

Open `<user>`'s crontab:

```
crontab -e
```

Add the line:

```
*/5 * * * * <install-dir>/scripts/orphan-ref-pruner
```


## Step 6: Verify the installation

Confirm the service is running:

```
systemctl status gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service
```

Expect `Active: active (running)`.

Trigger a manual sync to confirm end-to-end plumbing works:

```
<install-dir>/scripts/bidirectional-sync
```

Expect exit code 0 and no new `ERROR` lines in the activity log:

```
tail -20 <install-dir>/activity-log/activity.log
```

Within `HEARTBEAT_SECONDS` (12 hours by default), a heartbeat line should appear in that log reading `heartbeat: synchronizer is operational, local and Gitea repositories are in sync, orphaned commits have been pruned`.


## When something goes wrong

- **Service won't start:** `journalctl -u gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service -n 50`. Likely causes: a bad path in `ExecStart`, a missing or unreadable `/etc/gitea-sync.env`, or an unset required variable (`GITEA_URL`, `GITEA_USER`, `GITEA_TOKEN`, or `SCAN_ROOTS`) — the scripts abort immediately in that case.
- **Service is running but nothing appears to sync:** run `<install-dir>/scripts/bidirectional-sync` by hand and watch stderr. Also check `<install-dir>/activity-log/activity.log` for `ERROR` lines.
- **Gitea rejects pushes:** the token lacks `repo` write permission, is wrong, or has expired.
- **Repos aren't being picked up:** check `SCAN_ROOTS` in `/etc/gitea-sync.env` points at the right folder, that repo paths contain no spaces, and that repo names only contain `A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -`.
- **Auto-import into Gitea fails:** Gitea is blocking local-network migrations. Set `[migrations] ALLOW_LOCALNETWORKS = true` in `app.ini` and restart Gitea.
- **DB reconcile / pull-ref pruning does nothing:** the service user cannot access `GITEA_DB` and `GITEA_REPOS_DIR`. Re-check the group membership and permissions from the "If Gitea runs on the same box" section. If Gitea is on a different host, set both variables to empty strings.
- **Watcher silently stops picking up changes on a large tree:** `inotify` has hit `fs.inotify.max_user_watches`. Raise it, e.g. `echo 'fs.inotify.max_user_watches=524288' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/40-inotify.conf && sudo sysctl --system`.


## Uninstall

```
sudo systemctl stop    gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service
sudo systemctl disable gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/gitea-to-local-git-synchronizer-watcher.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
crontab -e    # and remove the orphan-ref-pruner line
sudo rm /etc/gitea-sync.env
rm -rf <install-dir>
```

Gitea's bare repositories and the user's working trees are left untouched.
